<UserControl x:Class="Bws.Gui.OverviewView"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:media="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Media;assembly=PresentationCore"
             Focusable="False"
             IsTabStop="False">

    <!--
        WHAT THIS MACHINE LOOKS LIKE BEFORE ANYBODY ASKS IT ANYTHING - `G`, the first-run screen.

        THE SPECIFICATION'S OWN REASON, because it is a better one than anything this file could
        invent: an administrator opens the tool on an unknown server and sees several hundred rows,
        and that is the moment they either understand what it is for or close it. So the first thing
        on screen is a handful of numbers, each of them a question the machine has already been
        asked.

        IT REPLACES THE LIST RATHER THAN SITTING OVER IT - owner's decision, 2026-08-25, and it is
        the difference between this and EmptyState.xaml next door. That one covers the middle of the
        grid and deliberately keeps the headings, because the rows come back on the next key press.
        This one is what the specification asks for in as many words - not the raw alphabetical
        list - so the grid is collapsed behind it and comes back the moment anybody asks a question.

        EVERY NUMBER IS A BUTTON AND CLICKING IT WRITES ITS QUERY INTO THE SEARCH BOX. That is `A5`'s
        promise applied to a screen with no chips on it: the filter you clicked is a query you can
        read, edit and learn from. Which questions there are, and why each one is worded the way it
        is, is decided in ViewModels/Overview.cs beside the reason for it - markup listing them
        again would be a second copy that drifts.

        Focusable and IsTabStop are off because a UserControl IS a ContentControl and a
        ContentControl is BOTH BY DEFAULT. Without them this is a tab stop that does nothing, in
        front of the numbers it holds. `docs/10` trap 8.

        NOT IsHitTestVisible="False", unlike the empty state, and the difference is the whole point:
        that panel must let clicks through to the rows underneath, and every line of this one is
        meant to be clicked.
    -->
    <UserControl.Resources>

        <!--
            ONE NUMBER AND WHAT IT COUNTS, AS A BUTTON THAT LOOKS LIKE A LINE OF TEXT.

            WRITTEN OUT RATHER THAN BasedOn, which is trap 4 of `docs/10` met head on and the same
            answer ScopeBar.xaml gives: this wants none of the library's chrome - no fill, no border,
            no corner radius - so inheriting a template and then hiding every part of it would be a
            worse lie than replacing it. The cost is named: every state this control has is this
            file's job, so hover and keyboard focus are both below.

            THE FOCUS RING IS A FocusVisualStyle RATHER THAN A TRIGGER ON IsKeyboardFocused, which
            is the repair made on 2026-08-25 to the scope switch and the reason is the same:
            IsKeyboardFocused says nothing about how the focus arrived, so clicking with a mouse lit
            the ring. A focus visual is drawn by the framework only while the keyboard is the device
            somebody is actually using.

            The transparent background is what makes the whole line take a click rather than only
            the letters - `docs/10`: a transparent brush takes hit testing, no brush does not.
        -->
        <Style x:Key="OverviewFocusRing">
            <Setter Property="Control.Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate>
                        <Border BorderBrush="{StaticResource FocusRing}"
                                BorderThickness="{StaticResource FocusRingThickness}" />
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <Style x:Key="OverviewNumber" TargetType="Button">
            <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{StaticResource OverviewFocusRing}" />
            <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
            <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadCell}" />

            <Setter Property="Template">
                <Setter.Value>
                    <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
                        <!--
                            Transparent through x:Static, which is the idiom ScopeBar.xaml and
                            Cells.xaml already use - a value pointing at a NAME rather than invented
                            in place, so `ADR-23` holds and the guard over it stays able to mean
                            something.
                        -->
                        <Border x:Name="Line"
                                Background="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}"
                                Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                            <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                        </Border>

                        <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                <Setter TargetName="Line" Property="Background"
                                        Value="{StaticResource SurfaceHover}" />
                            </Trigger>
                        </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                    </ControlTemplate>
                </Setter.Value>
            </Setter>
        </Style>

        <!--
            THE NUMBER ITSELF, RIGHT ALIGNED IN A FIXED COLUMN so that a column of them is
            comparable at a glance - complaint 3 of the eleven in `docs/11`, the same rule the
            process identifier column follows.
        -->
        <DataTemplate x:Key="OverviewRow">
            <Button Style="{StaticResource OverviewNumber}"
                    Click="AskThis"
                    ToolTip="{Binding Query}">
                <Grid>
                    <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="{StaticResource ColumnOverviewNumber}" />
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                    </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                               Text="{Binding Count}"
                               HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center">
                        <TextBlock.Style>
                            <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource HeadingText}">
                                <!--
                                    A QUALIFICATION IS QUIETER THAN THE NUMBER IT QUALIFIES, and the
                                    two are told apart by a property rather than by two collections -
                                    OverviewLine.Leading carries the argument. A line saying "and 4
                                    more are per-user templates" must not shout as loudly as the
                                    number it is taking four off.
                                -->
                                <Style.Triggers>
                                    <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Leading}" Value="False">
                                        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
                                        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightNormal}" />
                                        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
                                    </DataTrigger>
                                </Style.Triggers>
                            </Style>
                        </TextBlock.Style>
                    </TextBlock>

                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                               Text="{Binding Label}"
                               TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center"
                               Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}">
                        <TextBlock.Style>
                            <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource BodyTextBlockStyle}">
                                <Style.Triggers>
                                    <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Leading}" Value="False">
                                        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
                                    </DataTrigger>
                                </Style.Triggers>
                            </Style>
                        </TextBlock.Style>
                    </TextBlock>
                </Grid>
            </Button>
        </DataTemplate>
    </UserControl.Resources>

    <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
        <StackPanel MaxWidth="{StaticResource WidthOverview}"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                    Margin="{StaticResource PadPanel}">

            <TextBlock Text="{DynamicResource gui.overview.title}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Style="{StaticResource HeadingText}" />

            <TextBlock Text="{DynamicResource gui.overview.hint}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Margin="{StaticResource MarginAboveStatus}"
                       Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />

            <!--
                AN ItemsControl RATHER THAN SIX LINES WRITTEN OUT, exactly as the filters row and the
                scope switch do it. Which numbers there are is data, decided where the reason for
                each one lives.

                THE AutomationId IS ON THE ItemsControl AND NOT ON A PANEL, which this window has now
                paid for six times: WPF builds an automation peer for controls, not for layout
                panels, so an identifier on a StackPanel sits on an element that is not in the tree.
            -->
            <ItemsControl AutomationProperties.AutomationId="overviewNumbers"
                          AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource gui.overview.hint}"
                          Margin="{StaticResource MarginAboveStatus}"
                          ItemsSource="{Binding Overview}"
                          ItemTemplate="{StaticResource OverviewRow}" />

            <!--
                THE NUMBER `G` PROMISES AND THIS BUILD CANNOT COUNT, said rather than left off. It
                needs `D6`, the stock baseline, which the specification puts in Phase 3 - and R3 in
                that same document asks for exactly this: mark the absence rather than quietly show
                a bad result.
            -->
            <TextBlock AutomationProperties.AutomationId="overviewNotYet"
                       Text="{Binding OverviewMissing}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Margin="{StaticResource MarginAboveStatus}"
                       Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />

            <Button x:Name="DismissButton"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="overviewDismiss"
                    Content="{DynamicResource gui.overview.dismiss}"
                    ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.overview.dismiss.hint}"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                    Margin="{StaticResource MarginAboveStatus}"
                    Click="Dismiss" />
        </StackPanel>
    </ScrollViewer>
</UserControl>
